---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
---

<Base
  title="Data & API"
  description="CompactBench's public data: leaderboard JSON, families and season settings, Markdown pages, llms.txt and a remote MCP server for AI assistants."
  path="/data"
>
  <section class="container section prose">
    <p class="eyebrow">Data & API</p>
    <h1>Use the data</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      Everything on the leaderboard is available as plain files. No key, no sign-up, no rate limit
      beyond fair use. Licensed Apache 2.0.
    </p>

    <h2>JSON</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/api/leaderboard.json"><code>/api/leaderboard.json</code></a> — every published row: scores, intervals, per-family and per-compaction scores, settings.</li>
      <li><a href="/api/families.json"><code>/api/families.json</code></a> — the failure families.</li>
      <li><a href="/api/season.json"><code>/api/season.json</code></a> — the current season's pinned settings.</li>
      <li><code>/api/methods/&lt;slug&gt;.json</code> — one row, the same slug as its <code>/methods/&lt;slug&gt;</code> page.</li>
      <li><a href="/api/leaderboard.csv"><code>/api/leaderboard.csv</code></a> — the leaderboard as CSV, one column per family.</li>
      <li><a href="/api/openapi.json"><code>/api/openapi.json</code></a> — an OpenAPI 3.1 description of all of the above.</li>
    </ul>

    <h2>For AI assistants</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><a href="/llms.txt"><code>/llms.txt</code></a> and <a href="/llms-full.txt"><code>/llms-full.txt</code></a> — a map of the site, and every page in one file.</li>
      <li>Every content page has a Markdown twin: <a href="/methodology.md"><code>/methodology.md</code></a>, <a href="/leaderboard.md"><code>/leaderboard.md</code></a>, …</li>
      <li>
        A remote MCP server at <code>https://compactbench.com/mcp</code> (Streamable HTTP, read-only) with tools
        <code>get_leaderboard</code>, <code>get_method</code>, <code>compare_methods</code>,
        <code>list_families</code> and <code>explain_metric</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A self-test for AIs</strong>, on the same server: <code>selftest_list</code>,
        <code>selftest_turns</code>, <code>selftest_questions</code> and <code>selftest_score</code>. An
        assistant compacts a public CompactBench conversation with its own model, cycle by cycle, answers
        multiple-choice questions from its memory, and gets a score overall, after each compaction and per
        failure family, with what it missed. Scoring is mechanical, so nothing runs on our side. It's
        self-reported practice, not a leaderboard result.
      </li>
    </ul>
    <pre><code>{`# Add it to Claude Code
claude mcp add --transport http compactbench https://compactbench.com/mcp

# Then ask, for example:
#   Take the CompactBench self-test quick-decision-override, compacting the way you
#   normally would, and tell me what you forgot.`}</code></pre>

    <h2>Run it yourself</h2>
    <p>
      The benchmark is a Python package. Results files hold scores and settings only, so they're safe
      to share; traces hold everything and stay wherever you write them.
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`pip install compactbench
compactbench run --method built-in:hierarchical-summary --suite practice \\
  --provider anthropic --model claude-haiku-5-5 --reader-effort high --controls`}</code></pre>
  </section>
</Base>
